    <text type="letter">
        <body>
            <div type="writingSession">
                <pb n="1"/>
                <opener><fw place="top" type="letterhead">UNION THEOLOGICAL SEMINARY <lb/>BROADWAY
                        AT 120TH STREET <lb/><rs ref="#tillich_place_id__319" type="place">NEW
                            YORK</rs></fw><dateline><date when="1946-04-22">April 22.
                        1946</date></dateline>
                    <salute>Dear <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3469" type="person">Mrs.
                            Kotschnig</rs>:</salute></opener>
                <p>I am sorry that I answer so late but I have been out of town several times during
                    the last two weeks and was not able to dictate to my secretary.</p>
                <p>I have known <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3470" type="person">Mrs. Elkisch</rs>
                    since 1920 and estimate her very highly as a person from the point of view of
                    character and education. As a student of the University of <rs
                        ref="#tillich_place_id__37" type="place">Berlin</rs> she was considered
                    particularly gifted and highly thought of. She has had a Jungian analysis in <rs
                        ref="#tillich_place_id__37" type="place">Berlin</rs> and has even worked
                    with <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1006" type="person">Jung</rs> himself. She was
                    a lay analyst before she came to this country. Here she has had years of
                    experience in psychiatric treatment in some agencies or clinics. I believe that
                    she is particularly qualified for both therapy and teaching. From her
                        dissertation<note type="ea">Bei der unveröffentlichten Dissertation handelt
                        es sich um: <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3470" type="person">Elkisch,
                            Paula</rs>: Certain Projective Techniques as a Means of Investigating
                        the Psychodynamic Status of
                        Children<!--, doctoral dissertation, University of
                        Michigan-->.</note> – she got her Ph. D. in <rs
                        ref="#tillich_place_id__755" type="place">Michigan</rs> – and her
                    publications which I have seen I must say that she <supplied>is</supplied> also
                    a fascinating writer. With regard to leading a discussion group, I am sure that,
                    if she should like to do it, she would do well. She has given lectures here at
                    various places, I don&apos;t remember tho&apos; where, and was very successful.<pb
                        n="2"/></p>
                <p>I hope that this answers your questions.</p>
                <p>I envy <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1084" type="person">Walter</rs> that he could
                    go to <rs ref="#tillich_place_id__776" type="place">Austria</rs>. I am sorry
                    that neither <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1923" type="person">Mrs. Tillich</rs>
                    nor myself could attend the group meetings this winter. Always something
                    unforeseen happened when we intended to come. I hope this will be better next
                    winter.</p>
                <closer><salute>With best regards.</salute><signed>Sincerely yours,
                        <!-- keine Unterschrift --></signed>
                </closer>
                <!-- nach "Unterschrift" ein: <q>/rf</q>-->
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
